---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import HubPageShell from '../components/hub/HubPageShell.astro';
import { articleSchema } from '../lib/schema';

const nav = [
  { id: 'doors', label: 'Your situation' },
  { id: 'methods', label: 'The methods' },
  { id: 'start', label: 'Start here' },
  { id: 'core', label: 'Core questions' },
  { id: 'orient', label: 'Where to read next' },
  { id: 'more', label: 'More questions' },
  { id: 'before', label: 'Questions before choosing' },
];

const pageDescription =
  'Repair methods explained as jobs, not products: helical piers, micropiles, push piers, compaction grouting, foam jacking, and other deep foundations. A method name is not a diagnosis. The right tool matches the load path, the movement, the soil, and the water.';

const schema = articleSchema({
  headline: 'Repair Methods',
  description: pageDescription,
  url: 'https://www.myfoundationrepairconsultant.com/repair-methods',
  faqs: [
    { name: 'Does my foundation need deeper support?', text: 'That describes a job, not yet a method. Ask what load must move, where it must go, and why deeper support is needed. Helical piers, push piers, and micropiles may apply when the question is a structural underpinning load path.' },
    { name: 'Is the soil under my house the problem?', text: 'Soil can mean moisture-sensitive ground, loose fill, loss of support, erosion, or something else. Ask what condition was identified and how. Compaction grouting and, in some cases, foam jacking or other ground-improvement systems may apply.' },
    { name: 'Is there a void under my slab?', text: 'A void can matter a great deal — or be only one part of the story. Ask what created it and whether the source is still active. Foam jacking and compaction grouting are the usual places to start reading.' },
    { name: 'Can this be lifted?', text: 'Lift and stabilization are different jobs. Ask whether lift is necessary, how much is intended, what could limit it, and what will be monitored. Foam jacking is often discussed for slabs. Helical or push piers may be discussed when a footing itself is being lifted.' },
    { name: 'Does "bedrock" automatically mean the repair is sound?', text: 'No. "Bedrock" is not a complete design explanation. What material was encountered, at what depth, whether it is competent for the intended load, how the system engages it, and how the existing building connects to the proposed support all matter.' },
    { name: 'Does a deeper repair automatically mean a better repair?', text: 'No. A deeper system may be appropriate where shallow support is inadequate, but depth alone does not solve a moisture problem, a drainage problem, a structural-connection problem, a void, or an incorrect diagnosis.' },
    { name: 'Can compaction grouting and foam do the same thing?', text: 'Their scopes can overlap around voids, loose ground, slab support, or controlled lifting, but the materials and mechanisms differ. Compaction grouting uses stiff low-mobility grout to displace and densify ground; polyurethane injection uses expanding resin to fill voids and potentially lift or improve support.' },
    { name: 'Why might a contractor propose one method but not another?', text: 'The reason may be legitimate — access, equipment size, building weight, footing condition, vibration limits, depth of weak soil, obstructions, soil type, water conditions, desired lift, or cost. The owner should still ask what alternatives were considered and why they were ruled out.' },
    { name: 'Can methods be combined?', text: 'Yes. A project may combine drainage correction, underpinning, void filling, soil improvement, monitoring, or slab repair — but a combination should be tied to distinct identified problems, not assumed to be more thorough by default.' },
    { name: 'What is the proposed load path?', text: 'How load moves from the existing footing, wall, column, or slab into the proposed repair, and then into the ground.' },
    { name: 'What is the existing foundation actually capable of carrying?', text: 'A high-capacity pier does not automatically make a weak, narrow, cracked, poorly reinforced, or eccentric footing suitable for concentrated bracket loads.' },
  ],
});
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Repair Methods"
  description={pageDescription}
  hub={true}
  quote="The right repair is not the loudest tool, the deepest tool, or the cheapest tool. It is the method that matches a demonstrated problem and a clearly explained objective."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  <HubPageShell
    title="Repair Methods"
    subhead="What the names mean — and what they do not prove"
    description="A method name is not a diagnosis. Piers, grout, and foam can each be useful in the right setting. The choice should follow the building’s load path, movement pattern, soil conditions, water conditions, access, and repair objective."
    glance={[
      { label: 'Start here', value: 'What job is being asked of the method?' },
      { label: 'Then', value: 'What evidence supports that job?' },
      { label: 'Compare only likes', value: 'Piers with piers. Ground improvement with ground improvement.' },
      { label: 'Not this page', value: 'A ranking of products' },
    ]}
    nav={nav}
    topicsStyle="list"
  >
    <h2 class="section-heading" id="doors">Your situation</h2>
    <p class="door-lead">Tap a question that matches what you are trying to understand. The six method names stay visible below whether you tap or not.</p>

    <div class="door-grid">
      <label class="door-card">
        <input type="checkbox" class="door-toggle" />
        <span class="door-face door-front">
          <span class="door-hint">Question</span>
          <span class="door-q">Does my foundation need deeper support?</span>
          <span class="door-cue">Tap for the better question</span>
        </span>
        <span class="door-face door-back">
          <span class="door-hint">Better question</span>
          <span class="door-a">That describes a job, not yet a method. Ask what load must move, where it must go, and why deeper support is needed.</span>
          <span class="door-links">
            <a href="/helical-piers">Helical Piers →</a>
            <a href="/push-piers">Push Piers →</a>
            <a href="/micropiles">Micropiles →</a>
          </span>
          <span class="door-cue">Tap to close</span>
        </span>
      </label>

      <label class="door-card">
        <input type="checkbox" class="door-toggle" />
        <span class="door-face door-front">
          <span class="door-hint">Question</span>
          <span class="door-q">Is the soil under my house the problem?</span>
          <span class="door-cue">Tap for the better question</span>
        </span>
        <span class="door-face door-back">
          <span class="door-hint">Better question</span>
          <span class="door-a">Soil can mean moisture-sensitive ground, loose fill, loss of support, erosion, or something else. Ask what condition was identified and how.</span>
          <span class="door-links">
            <a href="/compaction-grouting">Compaction Grouting →</a>
            <a href="/foam-jacking">Foam Jacking →</a>
            <a href="/other-deep-foundations">Other Deep Foundations →</a>
          </span>
          <span class="door-cue">Tap to close</span>
        </span>
      </label>

      <label class="door-card">
        <input type="checkbox" class="door-toggle" />
        <span class="door-face door-front">
          <span class="door-hint">Question</span>
          <span class="door-q">Is there a void under my slab?</span>
          <span class="door-cue">Tap for the better question</span>
        </span>
        <span class="door-face door-back">
          <span class="door-hint">Better question</span>
          <span class="door-a">A void can matter a great deal — or be only one part of the story. Ask what created it and whether the source is still active.</span>
          <span class="door-links">
            <a href="/foam-jacking">Foam Jacking →</a>
            <a href="/compaction-grouting">Compaction Grouting →</a>
          </span>
          <span class="door-cue">Tap to close</span>
        </span>
      </label>

      <label class="door-card">
        <input type="checkbox" class="door-toggle" />
        <span class="door-face door-front">
          <span class="door-hint">Question</span>
          <span class="door-q">Can this be lifted?</span>
          <span class="door-cue">Tap for the better question</span>
        </span>
        <span class="door-face door-back">
          <span class="door-hint">Better question</span>
          <span class="door-a">Lift and stabilization are different jobs. Ask whether lift is necessary, how much is intended, what could limit it, and what will be monitored.</span>
          <span class="door-links">
            <a href="/foam-jacking">Foam Jacking →</a>
            <a href="/helical-piers">Helical Piers →</a>
            <a href="/push-piers">Push Piers →</a>
          </span>
          <span class="door-cue">Tap to close</span>
        </span>
      </label>
    </div>

    <p class="mercy-line" id="mercy">
      See a name you don’t recognize — drilled shaft, caisson, CFA, H-pile, sheet pile, Geopier?
      <a href="/other-deep-foundations">Other Deep Foundations →</a>
    </p>

    <h2 class="section-heading" id="methods">The methods</h2>
    <div class="condition-grid">
      <div class="condition-card" id="helical">
        <h4>Helical Piers</h4>
        <p>Steel elements installed by rotation to provide a deep-foundation load path. Not an automatic answer to every crack or moisture-related movement.</p>
        <div class="related-links"><a href="/helical-piers">Helical Piers →</a></div>
      </div>
      <div class="condition-card" id="micropiles">
        <h4>Micropiles</h4>
        <p>Small-diameter drilled-and-grouted reinforced foundation elements. Used for structural support and, in some work, in-place earth reinforcement. Not simply “stronger piers.”</p>
        <div class="related-links"><a href="/micropiles">Micropiles →</a></div>
      </div>
      <div class="condition-card" id="push">
        <h4>Push Piers</h4>
        <p>Steel resistance piers pressed using an existing structure as installation reaction. Not torque-installed helical piers. Not automatically suitable for a light structure or an unresolved water problem.</p>
        <div class="related-links"><a href="/push-piers">Push Piers →</a></div>
      </div>
      <div class="condition-card" id="grout">
        <h4>Compaction Grouting</h4>
        <p>Stiff, low-mobility grout used to displace and densify suitable ground. Not foam. Not a conventional deep pile.</p>
        <div class="related-links"><a href="/compaction-grouting">Compaction Grouting →</a></div>
      </div>
      <div class="condition-card" id="foam">
        <h4>Foam Jacking</h4>
        <p>Expanding polyurethane used to fill voids, restore contact, and lift concrete in controlled stages. Not a pile. In suitable documented conditions it can be used for more than cosmetic slab lifting, and it can be a reasonable alternative to a pier proposal when the problem is treatable soil or voiding and a separate deep load path is not required.</p>
        <div class="related-links"><a href="/foam-jacking">Foam Jacking →</a></div>
      </div>
      <div class="condition-card" id="other">
        <h4>Other Deep Foundations</h4>
        <p>A vocabulary guide to systems commonly encountered in commercial, industrial, infrastructure, marine, retaining, and new-construction work. Not a sixth residential repair product.</p>
        <div class="related-links"><a href="/other-deep-foundations">Other Deep Foundations →</a></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="ask-box" id="start">
      <h3>Start here</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Is the goal deep load transfer, soil improvement, void filling, slab lifting, or retaining soil?</li>
        <li>Is the movement active, historic, seasonal, localized, or widespread?</li>
        <li>Have drainage, irrigation, plumbing, erosion, and soil-moisture change been considered?</li>
        <li>What information supports the proposed method?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A method is only as good as the job it is asked to do. Sometimes the quieter option is the better one. Soil, water, access, structure weight, scope, and cost can make a simpler method the honest answer.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="section-heading" id="core">Core questions</h2>
    <div class="content-block">
      <h3>What problem is the method meant to solve?</h3>
      <p>A proposal should identify whether the intended job is:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Transfer structural load deeper.</li>
        <li>Improve shallow or intermediate-depth soil.</li>
        <li>Fill voids and restore contact beneath a slab.</li>
        <li>Lift a slab or foundation toward a target elevation.</li>
        <li>Retain soil, control erosion, or support an excavation.</li>
        <li>Support new construction or an addition.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A method can be technically sound and still be wrong for the stated problem.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-block">
      <h3>Which repair methods are actually comparable?</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Helical piers, push piers, and micropiles may be compared when the question is how to create a structural underpinning load path.</li>
        <li>Compaction grouting, rammed aggregate piers, and certain resin-injection approaches may be compared when the question is whether ground improvement can create adequate support.</li>
        <li>Foam jacking and cementitious slabjacking may be compared when the question is void filling, slab support, or controlled slab elevation.</li>
        <li>A project can require more than one category: underpinning for a footing and void filling beneath an adjacent slab, for example.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="content-block">
      <h3>Is the goal stabilization or lift?</h3>
      <p>Stabilizing movement and lifting a structure are related but different objectives. Lift can close gaps or restore elevations. It can also stress finishes, plumbing, brittle masonry, utilities, and parts of the structure that are not moving uniformly.</p>
      <p>A proposal should state whether lift is necessary, what degree of lift is intended, what could limit it, and what will be monitored during the work.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-block">
      <h3>Is water part of the mechanism?</h3>
      <p>Water is not automatically the cause of movement. It should not be treated as an afterthought. Roof runoff, surface drainage, irrigation, plumbing leaks, erosion, perched water, groundwater, and moisture-sensitive soil can change support conditions.</p>
      <p>A deep foundation system may still be appropriate when water is involved. The proposal should explain whether the water mechanism has been addressed, controlled, or intentionally separated from the structural-repair scope.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="section-heading" id="orient">Where to read next</h2>
    <p>These are orientation cards. They do not diagnose a house.</p>
    <div class="condition-grid">
      <div class="condition-card">
        <h4>Was I told the footing needs deeper support?</h4>
        <div class="related-links">
          <a href="/helical-piers">Helical Piers →</a>
          <a href="/push-piers">Push Piers →</a>
          <a href="/micropiles">Micropiles →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="condition-card">
        <h4>Was I told the slab has a void or has settled?</h4>
        <div class="related-links">
          <a href="/foam-jacking">Foam Jacking →</a>
          <a href="/compaction-grouting">Compaction Grouting →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="condition-card">
        <h4>Was I told the soil itself needs improvement?</h4>
        <div class="related-links">
          <a href="/compaction-grouting">Compaction Grouting →</a>
          <a href="/foam-jacking">Foam Jacking →</a>
          <a href="/other-deep-foundations">Other Deep Foundations →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="condition-card">
        <h4>Did the proposal use a name I do not recognize?</h4>
        <div class="related-links"><a href="/other-deep-foundations">Other Deep Foundations →</a></div>
      </div>
      <div class="condition-card">
        <h4>Was the problem identified after rain, irrigation, a leak, or erosion?</h4>
        <p>Start with water and soil behavior before selecting a repair system.</p>
        <div class="related-links">
          <a href="/material-behavior">Material Behavior →</a>
          <a href="/common-signs">Common Signs →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="section-heading" id="more">More questions</h2>
    <div class="accordion-section">
      <div class="accordion-header"><span class="accordion-title">Does “bedrock” automatically mean the repair is sound?</span><span class="accordion-icon">▸</span></div>
      <div class="accordion-content"><div class="accordion-content-inner content-block"><p>No. “Bedrock” is not a complete design explanation. Ask what material was encountered, at what depth, whether it is competent for the intended load, how the system engages it, and how the existing building connects to the proposed support.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="accordion-section">
      <div class="accordion-header"><span class="accordion-title">Does a deeper repair automatically mean a better repair?</span><span class="accordion-icon">▸</span></div>
      <div class="accordion-content"><div class="accordion-content-inner content-block"><p>No. A deeper system may be appropriate where shallow support is inadequate. Depth alone does not solve a moisture problem, a drainage problem, a structural-connection problem, a void, or an incorrect diagnosis.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="accordion-section">
      <div class="accordion-header"><span class="accordion-title">Can compaction grouting and foam do the same thing?</span><span class="accordion-icon">▸</span></div>
      <div class="accordion-content"><div class="accordion-content-inner content-block"><p>Sometimes their scopes overlap around voids, loose ground, slab support, or controlled lifting. Their materials and intended mechanisms are different. Compaction grouting uses stiff low-mobility grout to displace and densify suitable ground. Polyurethane injection uses expanding resin that can fill voids, create confinement, and potentially lift or improve support.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="accordion-section">
      <div class="accordion-header"><span class="accordion-title">Why might a contractor propose one method but not another?</span><span class="accordion-icon">▸</span></div>
      <div class="accordion-content"><div class="accordion-content-inner content-block"><p>The reason may be legitimate: access, equipment size, building weight, footing condition, vibration limits, depth of weak soil, obstructions, soil type, water conditions, desired lift, or cost.</p><p>The owner should still ask what alternatives were considered and why they were ruled out.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="accordion-section">
      <div class="accordion-header"><span class="accordion-title">Can methods be combined?</span><span class="accordion-icon">▸</span></div>
      <div class="accordion-content"><div class="accordion-content-inner content-block"><p>Yes. A project may combine drainage correction, underpinning, void filling, soil improvement, monitoring, or slab repair. A combination is not automatically more complete. It should be tied to distinct identified problems.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="accordion-section">
      <div class="accordion-header"><span class="accordion-title">What is the proposed load path?</span><span class="accordion-icon">▸</span></div>
      <div class="accordion-content"><div class="accordion-content-inner content-block"><p>Ask how load moves from the existing footing, wall, column, or slab into the proposed repair and then into the ground.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="accordion-section">
      <div class="accordion-header"><span class="accordion-title">What is the existing foundation actually capable of carrying?</span><span class="accordion-icon">▸</span></div>
      <div class="accordion-content"><div class="accordion-content-inner content-block"><p>A high-capacity pier does not automatically make a weak, narrow, cracked, poorly reinforced, or eccentric footing suitable for concentrated bracket loads.</p></div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="ask-box" id="before">
      <h3>Questions to ask before choosing a method</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>What movement or support problem is this method intended to solve?</li>
        <li>What investigation supports that explanation?</li>
        <li>Is the goal stabilization, lifting, deep load transfer, soil improvement, void filling, retaining soil, or support for a new load?</li>
        <li>What is the load path from the building into the proposed system?</li>
        <li>What is outside the scope — drainage, leak repair, slab work, utilities, monitoring, engineering?</li>
        <li>How will success be defined: stabilization, achieved lift, restored slab support, reduced settlement risk, or cosmetic repair?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </HubPageShell>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .door-lead { max-width: var(--col); color: var(--text-muted); margin: -8px 0 24px; font-size: 17px; }
  .door-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 16px; margin: 0 0 28px; }
  .door-card { display: block; position: relative; background: #ececec; border: 1px solid #c8c8c8; border-left: 3px solid var(--chile); padding: 22px 22px 18px; cursor: pointer; min-height: 168px; transition: transform 0.2s ease, box-shadow 0.2s ease, border-color 0.2s ease, background 0.2s ease; }
  .door-card:hover { transform: translateY(-4px); border-color: #9a9a9a; box-shadow: 0 14px 26px -16px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #e4e4e4; }
  .door-toggle { position: absolute; opacity: 0; pointer-events: none; }
  .door-face { display: block; }
  .door-back { display: none; }
  .door-card:has(.door-toggle:checked) { background: #e8e8e8; }
  .door-card:has(.door-toggle:checked) .door-front { display: none; }
  .door-card:has(.door-toggle:checked) .door-back { display: block; }
  .door-hint, .door-cue { display: block; font-family: 'DM Mono', monospace; font-size: 10px; letter-spacing: 2px; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--text-muted); }
  .door-hint { margin-bottom: 10px; }
  .door-cue { margin-top: 16px; color: var(--teal); }
  .door-q { display: block; font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif; font-size: 22px; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.25; color: var(--text-dark); }
  .door-a { display: block; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--text-body); }
  .door-links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 10px 16px; margin-top: 14px; }
  .door-links a, .mercy-line a, .related-links a { color: var(--teal); text-decoration: none; font-size: 15px; }
  .door-links a:hover, .mercy-line a:hover, .related-links a:hover { text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }
  .mercy-line { max-width: var(--col); margin: 0 0 40px; padding: 16px 0 0; font-size: 16px; color: var(--text-body); }
  @media (max-width: 700px) { .door-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .door-card { min-height: 0; } }
</style>
